course: dictionary
lesson: 13457
title: Hulking: big and heavy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words paint a picture for you. «The hulking troll blocked the bridge, and the goats ran away.» Look at the clues: the troll blocks the bridge and the goats run. A troll that makes goats run must be very big and heavy, so hulking means very big and heavy.
explain: Hulking can also describe things. «A hulking old ship sat stuck in the mud.» A ship is not a creature, but the word still tells you it is huge and heavy. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? Big, heavy or bulky would all fit, so you are on the right track.
explain: Hulking is about size and weight, not kindness. It can feel a bit clumsy or frightening, as in «a hulking great bully». In everyday speech, «a hulking great» is an informal way of saying very large. A hulking shape is one you notice straight away because it is so big.
fact: Hulking means very big, heavy and bulky.
fact: Hulking often suggests something clumsy or a little frightening.
fact: Hulking is an adjective, a describing word, and it comes from the noun «hulk».
fact: «A hulking figure» means a large, heavy shape.
fact: «A hulking great» is an informal UK phrase meaning very big.
exercise: «The hulking giant stomped across the field.» What does hulking mean? | Soft and gentle | Tiny and quick | *Very big and heavy | Bright and shiny
exercise: «A hulking shape loomed at the door.» Find and copy the word that means huge and heavy. | door | shape | loomed | *hulking
exercise: «The hulking bus blocked the narrow road.» Why might the author choose hulking instead of small? | *Because the bus is big, heavy and awkward | Because the bus is kind and gentle | Because the bus is tiny | Because buses are always quiet
test: «The hulking machine groaned as it lifted the rocks.» What is the best meaning of hulking? | Small and silent | *Huge and heavy | Very polite | Wet and cold
test: Is hulking a word about size and weight, or about kindness? | Kindness | Speed | Colours | *Size and weight
exam: Ben says «hulking» means kind and gentle. Is he right? | *No, it means very big and heavy, not kind | No, it means tiny | Yes, because it is a nice word | Yes, it means soft and kind
